{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.595,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Parallel and Intersecting Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.09,
            0.385,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.5 Transversals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.123,
            0.848,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "We saw what happens when two lines intersect in different ways. Let us explore what happens when one line intersects two different lines."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.169,
            0.615,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.463,
                0.331,
                0.537,
                0.346
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.14"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.355,
            0.845,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 5.14, line t intersects lines l and m. t is called a **transversal**. Notice that 8 angles are formed when a line crosses a pair of lines."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.402,
            0.847,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "Is it possible for all the eight angles to have different measurements? Why, why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.461,
            0.655,
            0.485
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What about five different angles — 6, 5, 4, 3 and 2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.493,
            0.848,
            0.568
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 5.14, since ∠1 and ∠3 are vertically opposite angles, they are equal. Are there other pairs of vertically opposite angles? We can see that there are a total of four pairs of vertically opposite angles and in each pair, the angles are equal to each other."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.567,
            0.847,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": "Thus, when a transversal intersects two lines, it forms eight angles with a maximum of four distinct angle measures."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.628,
            0.512,
            0.654
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.6 Corresponding Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.662,
            0.848,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 5.14, we notice that the transversal t forms two sets of angles — one with line l and another with line m. There are angles in the first set that correspond to angles in the second set based on their position. ∠1 and $\\angle 5$ are called **corresponding angles**. Similarly, ∠2 and ∠6, ∠3 and ∠7, ∠4 and ∠8 are the corresponding angles formed when transversal t intersects lines l and m."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.783,
            0.259,
            0.804
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.802,
            0.848,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": "Draw a pair of lines and a transversal such that they form two distinct angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.808,
